---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

const reasons = [
  {
    title: 'I learn by breaking things',
    body: 'Reading that a race condition killed patients is one thing. Typing fast enough to trigger it yourself is another. The lessons that stuck with me came from things I broke and then had to understand, so every project here is built for you to break.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The best engineering lessons are buried in PDFs',
    body: 'Inquiry board reports are some of the most honest engineering writing there is. They are also 60-page documents almost nobody reads. Rebuilding each failure as something you can run turns those reports into something you can feel.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Explanations should let you change the input',
    body: 'Most explanations give you the answer and stop. The real understanding comes when you ask “what if?” and can try it. That goes for a rocket’s guidance software and for a ball thrown at 45°.',
  },
  {
    title: 'How we learn and build has changed',
    body: 'With AI as a pair programmer, one person can now build what used to take a team. I want to show what that really looks like, the speed and the wrong turns, instead of a polished highlight reel.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Building in public keeps me honest',
    body: 'Published code can be checked. Published claims can be corrected. A public log means I ship on schedule, cite my sources, and own my mistakes.',
  },
];

const principles = [
  ['Sources first', 'Every claim about a real incident traces to the official investigation or a primary source.'],
  ['Simplifications labelled', 'Simulations simplify. When they do, the post says where and why.'],
  ['Proof, not prose', 'Every bug ships with a test that fails, and every fix with a test that passes.'],
  ['Open by default', 'All code is MIT-licensed. Every episode is a git tag you can check out.'],
  ['Respect', 'Some of these failures cost lives. We study them to learn, never to mock the people involved.'],
];
---

<Base title="About" description="Who runs ParamLab, and why it exists.">
  <article class="prose-wrap">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="label">About · operator notes</p>
      <h1>Hi, I’m Ram.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Yes, it’s hidden in the name: pa<strong>ram</strong>. A parameter is the one value you change to see how a
        system behaves, and that’s the whole idea behind this lab.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I’m an engineer who learns best by building. ParamLab is where I do that in public: I pick a system, find the
        parameter that matters, push it until something interesting happens, and publish everything. That means the
        code, the write-up, the video, and the mistakes along the way.
      </p>

      <h2>Why I’m doing this</h2>
    </div>

    <ol class="reasons">
      {
        reasons.map((r, i) => (
          <li class="panel">
            <span class="label num">R{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <div>
              <h3>{r.title}</h3>
              <p>{r.body}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What you’ll find here</h2>
      <p>
        <strong><a href="/lab/">Post-Mortem Lab</a>:</strong> history’s most expensive engineering failures, from Ariane 5
        to CrowdStrike, rebuilt as small simulations. You run them, trigger the bug, apply the fix, and keep one lesson.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong><a href="/explore/">Show Me Why</a>:</strong> ideas from school science and maths, explained with
        interactive visuals and the code behind them. Change a line and see what changes. It’s made for students, and
        for anyone who never got the “why” the first time.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
    </div>

    <dl class="spec">
      {
        principles.map(([k, v]) => (
          <div>
            <dt class="label">{k}</dt>
            <dd>{v}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Who it’s for</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Developers</strong> who want to recognise failure patterns before they ship them.</li>
        <li><strong>Students</strong> who learn better by poking at something than by memorising it.</li>
        <li><strong>Teachers</strong> looking for demos they can put on a projector and change live.</li>
        <li><strong>Anyone curious</strong> about how things work, and how they stop working.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Say hello</h2>
      <p>
        Spotted a mistake, or have an incident or topic you want rebuilt? Open an issue or a discussion on <a
          href={SITE.github}>GitHub</a>. Corrections get credited. You can also follow along via <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .head { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 4.5rem) 0.5rem; }
  .reasons {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .reasons li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 3rem 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
  }
  .num { color: var(--lab); padding-top: 0.25rem; }
  .reasons h3 { margin: 0 0 0.35rem; font-size: 1.12rem; }
  .reasons p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); text-wrap: pretty; }
  .spec {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  .spec div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 12rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.4rem 1.2rem;
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .spec div:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .spec dt { color: var(--ink); padding-top: 0.2rem; }
  .spec dd { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .spec div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .reasons li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.3rem; }
  }
</style>
